<h1>Nunjucks</h1>

<p>
  This page is itself a <code>.njk</code> template. The content pipeline routes files
  by <strong>file extension</strong> — not by any config flag — so a <code>.njk</code>
  file under <code>content/</code> is evaluated by the built-in Nunjucks engine with the
  full site context (<code>site.*</code>, <code>page.*</code>, <code>pages</code>,
  <code>tags</code>, <code>collections</code>). What you read below is generated by the
  engine while it renders this very page.
</p>

<h2>Variables</h2>
<p>Interpolation uses double braces. This paragraph is a live example:</p>
<p>
  The site is called <strong>{{ site.title }}</strong>, and it currently contains
  <strong>{{ pages | length }}</strong> pages.
</p>
<p>To print a literal <code>{{ "{{ }}" }}</code>, quote it as a string:
<code>{{ "{{ \"{{ site.title }}\" }}" }}</code>.</p>

<h2>Filters</h2>
<p>Filters chain with a pipe and receive arguments in parentheses:</p>
{% raw %}
<pre><code class="language-njk">{{ name | upper }}
{{ 'zest' | capitalize }}
{{ price | round(2) }}</code></pre>
{% endraw %}
<p>Rendered: <code>{{ "zest" | upper }}</code>, <code>{{ "zest" | capitalize }}</code>.</p>

<h2>Tags</h2>
<p>Logic lives in <code>{% %}</code> tags. The <code>if</code> and <code>for</code>
tags below run against the build-time page list:</p>
{% raw %}
<pre><code class="language-njk">{% for post in pages %}{% if post.title %}
&lt;a href="{{ post.url }}"&gt;{{ post.title }}&lt;/a&gt;
{% endif %}{% endfor %}</code></pre>
{% endraw %}
<p>This page itself demonstrates a real <code>if</code>:</p>
{% if site.language == "en" %}
<p class="alert alert--info">Rendered by <code>{{ "{% if site.language == \"en\" %}" }}</code> — the condition is true.</p>
{% endif %}

<h2>Raw blocks</h2>
<p>To show template syntax verbatim (as in this page's code samples), wrap it in
<code>{% raw %}…{% endraw %}</code>:</p>
{% raw %}
<pre><code class="language-njk">{% raw %}
{{ post.title }} — not evaluated, shown verbatim
{% endraw %}</code></pre>
{% endraw %}
<p>Anything inside <code>{% raw %}…{% endraw %}</code> is emitted without evaluation.</p>

<h2>Layouts and includes</h2>
<p>
  Layouts are chosen the same way: a layout file named <code>default.njk</code> is
  routed to the Nunjucks engine, while <code>docs.zest.fsx</code> is evaluated as an
  F# script. This site uses F# layouts, so the Nunjucks body above was handed to the
  F# <code>docs</code> layout as its <code>content</code> string.
</p>
<p>Legacy partials are also supported in templates and layouts via the
<code>{{ include name }}</code> syntax, which pulls a file from the site's includes
dictionary.</p>

<h2>Compatibility modes</h2>
<p>
  The <code>NunjucksCompatibility</code> flag (default <code>zest</code>) enables
  Zest's extended filters and macros on top of stock Nunjucks. In strict mode
  (<code>liquid</code>/<code>11ty</code>) the Zest extensions are skipped and only
  the built-in filters remain — see <a href="configuration.html">configuration</a>.
</p>
